bibliograph-web (0.2.21-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.21 release: the feed generator stops paying catalogue-scan latency,
    and a slow feed page no longer answers 503.

    - `fetchFeedPage` asks each activity collection for its own ordered page and
      merges them, instead of one `collection IN (…)` query. With an `IN` list
      the planner reads every row of the three collections and sorts them; with
      three ordered branches it merges the index scans and stops after the
      requested rows. A `MATERIALIZED` page CTE keeps the Bookhive `hiveId`
      lookup on the emitted rows, never on the join input.
    - The feed generator takes its own pool (`feedPool()`) instead of the
      catalog pool, so the catalog's 5 s statement bound cannot abort a feed
      page — that turned every page into `503 feed database is unavailable`
      because a page took 8.5 s on the production catalogue.
    - Measured with `0018`'s corrected index: one public feed page 8 537 ms →
      31 ms, and the endpoint answers `200` again.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 17:26:00 +0000

bibliograph-web (0.2.20-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.20 release: catalog reads answer in milliseconds instead of seconds.

    - `searchEditions` expresses its text match as a union of index-backed
      candidate sets (title, description, contributor name, identifier value)
      joined back to `editions`, instead of one `OR` whose `EXISTS` branches
      forced a catalogue scan with a subplan per row. Each branch carries the
      relevance bucket the old `CASE` emitted, so `min(rank)` reproduces the
      same order; the exact match set and page content are unchanged. A
      language-only browse still takes the plain catalogue scan.
    - `searchContributors` and `searchPublishers` get the same treatment (name
      and identifier value as two joined sets), and their identifier branch
      skips rows whose `identifiers` array is empty, which is what the partial
      indexes serve.
    - `resolveEditions`, `searchEditions` and `listEditionsByContributor` no
      longer count a first page that is shorter than `limit`: its length is the
      exact total, so the second full pass over the predicate is skipped. The
      response contract does not change — `total` stays exact.
    - The catalog pool is 15 connections (`CATALOG_POOL_MAX`), with a 2 s
      acquire timeout and a 5 s statement bound, so a saturated or runaway
      query fails inside the request instead of queueing the whole surface
      behind five slots.
    - `bibliograph_catalog_queries_total` gains `outcome="pool_timeout"`, so a
      pool-acquire timeout is no longer indistinguishable from a SQL error.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sun, 15 Nov 2026 16:58:18 +0000

bibliograph-web (0.2.19-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * 0.2.19 release: per-identifier format claims, and a filtered contributor
    listing.

    - New anonymous query
      `net.olamaelcu.livtet.biblio.getIdentifiedFormats` (`uri`, an edition
      at-uri) answers `{ uri, chapters?, identifiers }`: one claim per
      identifier in the catalog's own rank order (isbn13, isbn10, asin, then
      any other issuer; ties by issuer then value). The edition record's
      `format`, `pages` and `duration_seconds` are independent rank unions, so
      three winners can describe a product no identifier claims; a reading
      position needs the medium and extent of one product. A missing or
      malformed `uri` is `InvalidRequest`, an unknown edition `RecordNotFound`.

    - `listEditionsByContributor` accepts the `sort`, `order` and `language`
      parameters `searchEditions` already takes, built from the same
      `editionOrderBy` / `editionLanguageClause` helpers: `title` sorts
      alphabetically, `publishedYear` by the shape claim's year with unknown
      years last, `relevance` keeps the listing's own newest-first order, and
      `total` counts what the filters returned.

    - Lexicons: `getIdentifiedFormats.json` and the `readingProgress` record
      schema are new, and `listEditionsByContributor.json` declares the new
      parameters. The edge build regenerates `xrpc-catalog.json` from them; the
      HappyView payload ships them and its deploy oneshot uploads them.

